{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"big-purchase-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Big purchase track","description":"Takes a large household purchase such as an appliance, furniture or a bike from need to a checked delivery in gated steps - brief, shortlist, true cost, buying safely and the first weeks of ownership.","category":"shopping","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","verify"],"role":["individual","parent"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","preferences","url"],"output":["plan","table","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["major-purchase","shortlist","total-cost","return-window","delivery-checks"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["decide-whether-to-buy","compare-purchase-options","time-a-purchase","decide-on-extended-warranty","check-online-shop-legitimacy"]},"args":[{"name":"purchase","description":"What you need to buy and why now, for example \"a fridge-freezer; ours is 15 years old and noisy\" or \"a sofa for our first flat\".","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"budget","description":"What you can spend without debt, with currency, and how soon you need it.","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"country","description":"Your country, since consumer rights, labels and delivery practices differ.","type":"string","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"brief","file":"steps/01-brief.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"shortlist","file":"steps/02-shortlist.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"true-cost","file":"steps/03-true-cost.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve"},{"id":"buy-safely","file":"steps/04-buy-safely.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve"},{"id":"after-delivery","file":"steps/05-after-delivery.md","stage":"verify","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Walks one large purchase from need to a checked delivery the way an independent purchase adviser would, pausing after each step for the person's approval. The brief comes first because most bad purchases start from a vague need.\n\nPurchase: {{purchase}}\nBudget: {{budget}}\nCountry: {{country}}\n\nThroughout: use the person's facts and the listings they paste. Never invent model names, specs, prices, review scores or shops, and never claim to have checked a price or a listing; say how to check. Legal rights for faulty goods, cancellations and returns differ by country: describe what typically applies and point to the official consumer-rights source. If the purchase is a phone, computer, car or financial product, say those need their own specialist checks and keep to the general logic. If the budget would need borrowing, say so plainly and offer to stop or scale down.","steps":[{"id":"brief","file":"steps/01-brief.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Write the brief\n\nPin down what the purchase must do before looking at products.\n\n1. Read the purchase, budget and country. If the use, the space or who will use it is unclear, ask up to four questions in one message and wait.\n2. Check the cheaper paths first: could the current item be repaired, borrowed, rented or bought used? Say in two or three lines whether any is worth trying.\n3. Write a one-paragraph brief: the job it must do, who uses it, where it goes, how long it should last, and the deadline.\n4. List must-haves (measurements, connections, capacity, safety needs) and nice-to-haves, and the two or three specs that will decide the choice.\n5. Note constraints: access for delivery (doors, stairs, lifts), what it must work with, and values such as secondhand or low energy use.\n\nStop and ask the person to approve or correct the brief before building a shortlist."},{"id":"shortlist","file":"steps/02-shortlist.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Build the shortlist\n\nTurn the brief into a shortlist the person can fill and compare.\n\n1. Explain where to look and how to search for this category (by the deciding specs, not by brand), and ask the person to paste two to five options they are considering, with their spec lines and prices.\n2. If they paste options, put them in a table against the must-haves; mark any option that fails a must-have as out, and anything unclear as \"verify\".\n3. If they have no options yet, give a fill-in table with the columns to use and the spec ranges to look for, without naming models.\n4. Point out marketing claims in the listings that need checking and what real evidence would look like.\n5. Say how to check reviews for this category: patterns across many reviews, failures after months rather than first impressions.\n\nStop and ask the person which two or three options go forward before working out the true cost."},{"id":"true-cost","file":"steps/03-true-cost.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Work out the true cost\n\nCompare the finalists on what they will really cost over their life.\n\n1. For each finalist, build a cost table: price, delivery, installation or assembly, removal of the old item, accessories and consumables, running costs from the energy or water label where relevant, and expected resale, using the person's figures or marked placeholders.\n2. Give a lifetime or per-year cost with the arithmetic shown, and label any lifespan as a rough typical range.\n3. Timing: is a better price likely soon, or is the need too urgent to wait? Suggest a target price and a buy-by date.\n4. Warranty: the manufacturer's warranty, the seller's legal responsibility for faults as typical and to verify, and whether any extended cover is worth looking at.\n5. Recommend one finalist, or a tie with the deciding question, and say what would change the choice.\n\nStop and ask the person to confirm the choice before planning the purchase."},{"id":"buy-safely","file":"steps/04-buy-safely.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Buy safely\n\nPlan the purchase itself so nothing goes wrong at the till or the door.\n\n1. Seller checks: signs the seller is legitimate, who handles warranty claims, and the return and cancellation terms for this item (opened or installed items can differ).\n2. Questions to ask before paying: delivery slot, access, installation and removal, what is included, and price matching.\n3. Payment: a method with buyer protection where available; avoid bank transfers to unknown sellers; if offered credit or instalments, show the total cost and say plainly when it is not worth it.\n4. Add-ons at checkout: what to decline and what to consider calmly at home.\n5. A one-page purchase record: order number, prices, terms, return deadline, warranty registration steps and contacts.\n\nStop and ask the person to confirm the order is placed, or what changed, before the delivery checks."},{"id":"after-delivery","file":"steps/05-after-delivery.md","stage":"verify","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Delivery and the first weeks\n\nMake sure the item is right while it is still easy to fix.\n\n1. Delivery checklist: inspect packaging and item before signing where possible, note damage on the delivery note, take photos, and check every part is there.\n2. First-use checks for this item type: what to test in the first days, and how long to let it settle where relevant.\n3. Admin: register the warranty, file the purchase record and manual, and diarise the return deadline.\n4. If something is wrong: who to contact first, what to say, and when it becomes a faulty-goods complaint rather than a return; point to the official consumer-rights source for their country.\n5. A short review: did it meet the brief, and one lesson for the next big purchase."}]}
